WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.080
For fans of Matt Groening's sci-fi comedy&nbsp;
"Futurama," it may seem like a millennium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.320
has come and gone since we last saw&nbsp;
the Planet Express crew in action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.240
It's hard to believe that a full 22 years have&nbsp;
passed since the show made its auspicious premiere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.520
on Fox and that it's been eight years since&nbsp;
its bittersweet finale brought it to a close.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.480
The series' fractured run saw it earn a handful&nbsp;
of Emmys, score a fiercely devoted fanbase, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.480
make a legitimate case for being one of the best&nbsp;
animated series of all time. Regardless, the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:41.200
constantly struggled with ratings and was canceled&nbsp;
by Fox after four glorious seasons, only to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:48.000
reborn on Comedy Central with the restructured&nbsp;
direct-to-video films. Then, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.720
it was canceled again after an additional two&nbsp;
seasons. Low ratings and cancelations aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.480
every step of the way, Groening and company&nbsp;
consistently made "Futurama" that rarest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.200
animated series — one that could easily make you&nbsp;
laugh out loud or openly weep into the cosmos.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.440
The show's blend of low-brow humor, high-minded&nbsp;
insight, and raw, unabashed emotion is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.240
helped make "Futurama" a cult hit in its initial&nbsp;
run on Fox. It's also a big reason the series'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:27.120
storied legacy only grew in syndication, which&nbsp;
ultimately led to its revival on Comedy Central.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.760
And it's why — even after that unexpected&nbsp;
gift of a 60 + episode revival — "Futurama"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.120
fans still clamor for even more cosmic&nbsp;
adventures with the Planet Express gang.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.760
We can't say for sure whether or not another&nbsp;
season of "Futurama" might eventually be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.960
the horizon. Though, with each passing day,&nbsp;
the prospect of new episodes full of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.080
heart, and random pop culture references seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.480
about as likely as ridding yourself&nbsp;
of a brain slug of your own free will.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.680
"Hopes deleted."

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.280
The only ray of optimism we can offer is that the&nbsp;
cast sounds excited to get back in the spaceship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.360
Voice actor Maurice LaMarche told Syfy Wire&nbsp;
that the old cast remains in touch and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.640
they're all ready and willing to head back to&nbsp;
the recording booth should more episodes arise.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.080
He went on to say…

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.320
"I have to leave it up to the Powers&nbsp;
That Be, but I know everybody's into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.040
including the cast. [...] So hopefully&nbsp;
someday there'll be a 'Futurama' reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.680
but we're well-practiced at the reboot thing."

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.400
Since the cast is willing, and now that&nbsp;
Disney acquired 20th Century Studios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.840
perhaps they will want to revive the show,&nbsp;
seeing how it still has a fervent fan base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.920
Stranger things have happened,&nbsp;
especially in the world of "Futurama."

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.320
"Guess this is your lucky day, Pimparoo."

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.440
However, some longtime viewers have&nbsp;
wondered whether we actually need new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.080
episodes of "Futurama." Those fans point&nbsp;
out that all good things do eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.040
come to an end — and many of the best things&nbsp;
come to an end a little quicker than most.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.480
That's especially true of&nbsp;
television. Thanks to poor ratings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.720
some brilliant shows with amazing&nbsp;
potential become history way too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.520
soon. And then there are others that stay&nbsp;
on the air many years past their prime.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.760
"I know what you mean."

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.680
Either way, most fans agree that "Futurama"&nbsp;
ended exactly the way it should have back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.240
in 2013. That's due to the fact that&nbsp;
Comedy Central's brass did something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.400
Fox didn't do so many years ago. They gave the&nbsp;
show's creators a heads-up that the end was near.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.360
That inside info allowed the "Futurama"&nbsp;
team to ensure that the series received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.640
fitting swan song — one that tugged on the&nbsp;
heartstrings as much as it tickled the funny bone.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.800
"Quit touching my junk, pervert!"

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.760
The show's finale — simply titled "Meanwhile" —&nbsp;
more than achieved that goal. Groening and company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.200
imbued the episode with all the wit, wonder,&nbsp;
and insight you'd expect from the series, icing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.760
the cake with a ravishingly romantic storyline&nbsp;
rarely seen in the annals of animated television.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.360
As many have pointed out, new&nbsp;
"Futurama" episodes might seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.920
extraneous in the wake of that finale.&nbsp;
Not just because "Meanwhile" was such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.200
an effective series end, but also because&nbsp;
fond farewells are so rare in an era where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.680
network and streaming executives always&nbsp;
seem to have their axes at the ready.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.200
After all, one of the key narrative threads&nbsp;
throughout the show's largely remarkable 140&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.640
episode run was that time is finite. No&nbsp;
matter how many time-shifting devices or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:41.040
bizarre scenarios you encounter, it's important to&nbsp;
appreciate what you have while you still have it.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.800
"No it isn't! Shut your filthy clam!"

